-- title:
--   Assumptions of Physics II.1: statements, experimental and theoretical domains, possibilities, natural topology
-- statement:
--   This definition file fixes the vocabulary of Part II, Chapter 1 of *Assumptions of Physics* (Carcassi–Aidala, v3.0).
--
--   **Statements as truth sets.** A logical context is represented by its set $\Omega$ of *possible assignments* (Axiom 1.4, Definition 1.5). Because the axiom of closure (Axiom 1.9, Proposition 1.14) makes every set of possible assignments the truth set of some statement, and two statements are equivalent (Definition 1.15) exactly when they are true in the same possible assignments, a statement up to equivalence is represented by its truth set $s\subseteq\Omega$. Negation, conjunction and disjunction become complement, intersection and union; the certainty is $\Omega$, the impossibility is $\emptyset$; "$s_1$ is narrower than $s_2$" is $s_1\subseteq s_2$; "$s_1$ is compatible with $s_2$" is $s_1\cap s_2\neq\emptyset$.
--
--   **Basis (Definition 1.34).** $B\subseteq\mathcal D$ is a basis of $\mathcal D$ if every element of $\mathcal D$ can be generated from $B$ by finite conjunction and countable disjunction (the empty conjunction and empty disjunction, i.e. certainty and impossibility, included).
--
--   **Experimental domain (Definition 1.35).** A set $\mathcal D$ of statements, closed under finite conjunction and countable disjunction, containing the certainty and the impossibility, and admitting a countable basis.
--
--   **Theoretical domain (Definition 1.36).** $\bar{\mathcal D}$, the closure of $\mathcal D$ under negation, finite conjunction and countable disjunction.
--
--   **Approximately verifiable (Definition 1.38).** $s\in\bar{\mathcal D}$ with $s=\bigcap_{i=1}^\infty s_i$ for some sequence $s_i\in\mathcal D$.
--
--   **Possibility (Definition 1.47).** $x\in\bar{\mathcal D}$, $x\not\equiv\bot$, such that for every $s\in\bar{\mathcal D}$ either $x\preccurlyeq s$ or $x$ is incompatible with $s$. The possibilities form the set (and type) $X$.
--
--   **Verifiable sets and natural topology (Definition 1.55, Proposition 1.57).** $U(s)=\{x\in X : x \text{ compatible with } s\}$; the natural topology on $X$ is the one generated by $\{U(s): s\in\mathcal D\}$ (Proposition 1.57, a separate milestone, asserts these are already all open sets).
--
--   **Decidable domain (Definition 1.73).** For every $s\in\mathcal D$ also $\neg s\in\mathcal D$.
--
--   **Minterm (Definition 1.25).** A conjunction in which every element of $B$ appears exactly once, negated or not.
--
--   The primitive notion of *verifiability* (Axiom 1.27) is not modelled separately: membership in $\mathcal D$ is what the chapter's mathematical results use.
-- source:
--   G. Carcassi, C. A. Aidala, Assumptions of Physics, Ver. 3.0 (Dec 31, 2025), https://assumptionsofphysics.org/book — Part II, Chapter 1 (pp. 101–146), Definitions 1.25, 1.34, 1.35, 1.36, 1.38, 1.47, 1.55, 1.73; Axioms 1.4, 1.9; Proposition 1.14 (pp. 101–142)

import Mathlib

/-!
# Assumptions of Physics, Part II, Chapter 1: experimental domains

Source: G. Carcassi, C. A. Aidala, *Assumptions of Physics*, Ver. 3.0 (December 31, 2025),
Part II ("Physical Mathematics"), Chapter 1 "Verifiable statements and experimental domains",
Sections 1.1–1.4 and 1.6.

Modelling convention. A logical context is represented by its type `Ω` of possible
assignments (Axiom 1.4, Definition 1.5). By the axiom of closure (Axiom 1.9 /
Proposition 1.14) every set of possible assignments is the truth set of some statement, and
two statements are equivalent (Definition 1.15) exactly when they are true in the same
possible assignments. A statement up to equivalence is therefore represented by its truth set
`s : Set Ω`; negation, conjunction and disjunction are complement, intersection and union;
the certainty is `Set.univ` and the impossibility is `∅`; `s₁ ≼ s₂` (narrower than) is
`s₁ ⊆ s₂`; `s₁` compatible with `s₂` is `(s₁ ∩ s₂).Nonempty`; incompatibility is `Disjoint`.
-/

namespace AssumptionsOfPhysics

universe u

/-- The statements generated from a set `B` of statements by finite conjunction and countable
disjunction (Definition 1.34). The empty conjunction (the certainty) and the empty disjunction
(the impossibility) are included. -/
inductive FinConjCountDisj {Ω : Type u} (B : Set (Set Ω)) : Set Ω → Prop
  | basic {s : Set Ω} : s ∈ B → FinConjCountDisj B s
  | univ : FinConjCountDisj B Set.univ
  | empty : FinConjCountDisj B ∅
  | inter {s t : Set Ω} : FinConjCountDisj B s → FinConjCountDisj B t →
      FinConjCountDisj B (s ∩ t)
  | iUnion (f : ℕ → Set Ω) : (∀ n, FinConjCountDisj B (f n)) →
      FinConjCountDisj B (⋃ n, f n)

/-- The statements generated from a set `B` of statements by negation, finite conjunction and
countable disjunction (Definition 1.36). The certainty (empty conjunction) is included. -/
inductive NegFinConjCountDisj {Ω : Type u} (B : Set (Set Ω)) : Set Ω → Prop
  | basic {s : Set Ω} : s ∈ B → NegFinConjCountDisj B s
  | univ : NegFinConjCountDisj B Set.univ
  | compl {s : Set Ω} : NegFinConjCountDisj B s → NegFinConjCountDisj B sᶜ
  | inter {s t : Set Ω} : NegFinConjCountDisj B s → NegFinConjCountDisj B t →
      NegFinConjCountDisj B (s ∩ t)
  | iUnion (f : ℕ → Set Ω) : (∀ n, NegFinConjCountDisj B (f n)) →
      NegFinConjCountDisj B (⋃ n, f n)

/-- Definition 1.34: `B ⊆ D` is a basis of the set of statements `D` if every element of `D`
can be generated from `B` using finite conjunction and countable disjunction. -/
def IsBasis {Ω : Type u} (D B : Set (Set Ω)) : Prop :=
  B ⊆ D ∧ ∀ s ∈ D, FinConjCountDisj B s

/-- Definition 1.35: an experimental domain is a set of statements, closed under finite
conjunction and countable disjunction, that contains the certainty and the impossibility and
is generated from a countable basis. The elements of `stmts` are the verifiable statements of
the domain. -/
structure ExperimentalDomain (Ω : Type u) where
  /-- The statements of the domain (as truth sets over the possible assignments `Ω`). -/
  stmts : Set (Set Ω)
  /-- The certainty belongs to the domain. -/
  univ_mem : Set.univ ∈ stmts
  /-- The impossibility belongs to the domain. -/
  empty_mem : ∅ ∈ stmts
  /-- Closure under finite (binary) conjunction. -/
  inter_mem : ∀ s t : Set Ω, s ∈ stmts → t ∈ stmts → s ∩ t ∈ stmts
  /-- Closure under countable disjunction. -/
  iUnion_mem : ∀ f : ℕ → Set Ω, (∀ n, f n ∈ stmts) → (⋃ n, f n) ∈ stmts
  /-- The domain admits a countable basis. -/
  exists_countable_basis : ∃ B : Set (Set Ω), B.Countable ∧ IsBasis stmts B

namespace ExperimentalDomain

variable {Ω : Type u} (D : ExperimentalDomain Ω)

/-- Definition 1.36: the theoretical domain `D̄`, the set of all statements generated from
`D` using negation, finite conjunction and countable disjunction. -/
def theoretical : Set (Set Ω) := {s | NegFinConjCountDisj D.stmts s}

/-- Definition 1.38: a theoretical statement is approximately verifiable if it is the
countable conjunction of a sequence of verifiable statements of the domain. -/
def IsApproxVerifiable (s : Set Ω) : Prop :=
  s ∈ D.theoretical ∧ ∃ f : ℕ → Set Ω, (∀ i, f i ∈ D.stmts) ∧ s = ⋂ i, f i

/-- Definition 1.47: a possibility for `D` is a theoretical statement `x` that is not
impossible and such that, for every theoretical statement `s`, either `x` is narrower than
`s` or `x` is incompatible with `s`. -/
def IsPossibility (x : Set Ω) : Prop :=
  x ∈ D.theoretical ∧ x.Nonempty ∧ ∀ s ∈ D.theoretical, x ⊆ s ∨ Disjoint x s

/-- Definition 1.47: the (full) possibilities `X` of `D`, as a set of statements. -/
def possibilities : Set (Set Ω) := {x | D.IsPossibility x}

/-- The possibilities of `D`, as a type: the points of the natural topology. -/
structure Possibility where
  /-- The statement underlying the possibility. -/
  val : Set Ω
  /-- It is a possibility of `D`. -/
  isPossibility : D.IsPossibility val

/-- Definition 1.55: the verifiable set `U(s) = {x ∈ X | x compatible with s}` of
possibilities associated with a statement `s`. -/
def verifiableSet (s : Set Ω) : Set D.Possibility :=
  {x | (x.val ∩ s).Nonempty}

/-- Proposition 1.57 / Definition 1.55: the natural topology on the possibilities, generated by
the verifiable sets `U(s)` of the statements `s ∈ D`. (That these sets are already *all* the
open sets is Proposition 1.57, stated separately.) -/
instance naturalTopology : TopologicalSpace D.Possibility :=
  TopologicalSpace.generateFrom (D.verifiableSet '' D.stmts)

/-- Definition 1.73: an experimental domain is decidable if for every `s ∈ D` also `¬s ∈ D`. -/
def IsDecidable : Prop := ∀ s ∈ D.stmts, sᶜ ∈ D.stmts

end ExperimentalDomain

/-- Definition 1.25: the minterm of a set of statements `B` selected by `σ`: the conjunction
over all `b ∈ B` of `b` (if `σ b = true`) or `¬b` (if `σ b = false`). -/
def minterm {Ω : Type u} (B : Set (Set Ω)) (σ : B → Bool) : Set Ω :=
  ⋂ b : B, if σ b then (b : Set Ω) else (b : Set Ω)ᶜ

end AssumptionsOfPhysics


